From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:53:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: imx: block transfers once the system shuts down

A reboot does not stop I2C traffic. Kernel threads and userspace keep
issuing transfers through device_shutdown() until the remaining CPUs are
stopped, and the reset can land while the controller is clocking a read.
The addressed target is then left driving a 0 data bit on SDA with SCL
released high, waiting for clock edges that never come. Targets without a
reset pin, and with an SMBus timeout that only watches SCL, stay that way
across every warm reset until power is removed. The bus is wedged for the
next boot: the controller sees it busy and reports arbitration lost on the
first START.

On LS1046A this is not recoverable from the SoC side for IIC1, whose SCL
has no GPIO alternate function for the nine-clock recovery. It has been
seen on an EMC2302 fan controller and on SFP modules behind PCA9545 muxes.

Add a shutdown callback that marks the adapter suspended, as the cadence
and qcom-geni drivers do. The helper takes the root adapter lock, so a
transfer in flight on another CPU completes first, and the core rejects
every later transfer with -ESHUTDOWN. Transfers through mux children go
through the parent adapter and are covered too.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
index a2afcf2d8ae4..ea30d318eb3c 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
@@ -2072,6 +2072,20 @@ static void i2c_imx_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	pm_runtime_disable(&pdev->dev);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A warm reset that lands mid-read leaves the addressed target driving a 0
+ * data bit on SDA, waiting for SCL edges that never come. Targets without a
+ * reset pin or an SDA timeout keep the bus wedged until power is removed.
+ * Marking the adapter suspended takes the bus lock, so any in-flight
+ * transfer completes first, and the core rejects every later one.
+ */
+static void i2c_imx_shutdown(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct imx_i2c_struct *i2c_imx = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
+
+	i2c_mark_adapter_suspended(&i2c_imx->adapter);
+}
+
 static int i2c_imx_runtime_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct imx_i2c_struct *i2c_imx = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
@@ -2165,6 +2179,7 @@ static const struct dev_pm_ops i2c_imx_pm_ops = {
 static struct platform_driver i2c_imx_driver = {
 	.probe = i2c_imx_probe,
 	.remove = i2c_imx_remove,
+	.shutdown = i2c_imx_shutdown,
 	.driver = {
 		.name = DRIVER_NAME,
 		.pm = pm_ptr(&i2c_imx_pm_ops),
-- 
